Accessibility Development Specification for ChatHierarchy Nav
Introduction
This specification outlines the accessibility requirements and implementation guidelines for the ChatHierarchy Nav Chrome extension, focusing on support for visually impaired users via screen readers like VoiceOver on macOS. The goal is to make the plugin fully operable without sighted assistance, enabling users to navigate the Timeline (Chronicle View) and Logical (Insight View) outlines, jump to corresponding ChatGPT conversation sections, and receive auditory feedback on actions. This addresses the usability challenges of VoiceOver by providing structured, predictable interactions that bridge visual jumps to auditory focus shifts.
Key principles follow WCAG 2.2 (AA level) and ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG), emphasizing:

Perceivable: Content is readable by screen readers.
Operable: Full keyboard and voice navigation, with focus shifts for jumps.
Understandable: Clear announcements for changes (e.g., jumps).
Robust: Compatibility with VoiceOver, using semantic HTML and ARIA.

The extension's tree-like structure (headlines and keypoints) will be treated as an ARIA tree view, allowing hierarchical navigation. Users will receive announcements like "Jumped to conversation section: Debugging errors" and optional TTS reading of selected GPT content. Fullscreen mode will define summaries as top-level headings for quick rotor access.
Requirements
Functional Requirements

Navigation Support: Users must navigate the outline using VoiceOver keyboard commands (e.g., VO + arrows for sibling nodes, VO + Shift + Up/Down for parent/child levels, VO + Space to select/expand).
Jump Feedback: Upon selecting a node, announce the jump (e.g., "Navigated to conversation turn 3: Project overview") and confirm the ChatGPT page has scrolled to the relevant content, shifting focus for immediate reading.
Content Reading Assistance: When a node is selected, provide an option to read the summarized keypoints or full GPT response via TTS. For long content, start with a summary and allow users to request more details. In fullscreen mode, summaries act as top-level headings for quick jumps to underlying content.
Mode Toggle: Include a "Voice Mode" toggle (accessible via keyboard) to enable automatic TTS announcements and voice commands.
Error Handling: Announce errors (e.g., "No matching node found") via aria-live regions.
Conformance: Ensure VoiceOver rotor (VO + U) lists tree items/headings for quick access, with fullscreen summaries as navigable top-level elements.

Non-Functional Requirements

Performance: Announcements should be immediate (<1s delay) to avoid disorientation.
Privacy: All processing local; no data sent externally.
Size/Impact: Keep ARIA additions lightweight to not bloat extension size.
Conformance: Achieve WCAG 2.2 AA: Perceivable (1.4.1 Color, 1.4.3 Contrast), Operable (2.1.1 Keyboard, 2.4.7 Focus Visible), Understandable (3.3.2 Labels), Robust (4.1.2 Name/Role/Value).

Implementation Guidelines
ARIA Roles, States, and Properties
Implement the outline as an ARIA tree view for hierarchical navigation, ensuring VoiceOver recognizes levels and states:

Container: Use <ul role="tree" aria-label="Conversation Outline"> for the main outline (Timeline or Logical View).
Nodes: Each headline/keypoint as <li role="treeitem" aria-level="1" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Headline: 项目概述, 3 keypoints available, press VO + Space to expand or jump">.

For sub-keypoints: aria-level="2".
Expansion state: Update aria-expanded="true/false" dynamically on toggle.


Selection/Focus: Use aria-selected="true" for selected nodes (if multi-select enabled). Ensure focusable with tabindex="0".
Update Announcements: Use a hidden <div role="status" aria-live="polite"> for feedback (e.g., innerText = "Jumped to conversation turn 3: Debugging errors").

aria-live="polite": Announces when user is idle, avoiding interruptions.


Labels: Every interactive element (buttons, toggles) must have aria-label or visible text (e.g., <button aria-label="Toggle Voice Mode">).

Keyboard Navigation (Integrated with VoiceOver)
Align with ARIA Tree View Pattern and VoiceOver's default behavior for vertical orientation. VoiceOver distinguishes flat and hierarchical navigation to allow users control over depth:

Flat/Sibling Navigation: VO + Right/Left/Up/Down Arrow: Move between sibling nodes at the current level (e.g., from one headline to the next in Timeline View). This is the primary way to browse peer items without entering sub-levels.
Hierarchical Navigation: VO + Shift + Down Arrow: Enter a group or expand to child nodes (e.g., from headline to its keypoints). VO + Shift + Up Arrow: Exit to parent or collapse.

This design allows users to "drill down" only when needed, reducing cognitive load in complex trees.


Interaction/Selection: VO + Space: Activate/select a node, trigger jump, and announce.
Rotor Integration: VO + U: Open the VoiceOver rotor menu to list headings, tree items, or links for quick quick jumps (e.g., select "Tree Items" in rotor, then arrows to navigate).
Global Shortcuts: Tab/Shift+Tab: Cycle through focusable elements (e.g., from view toggle to outline nodes). Home/End: Jump to first/last node in the tree.
JS Implementation: Add event listeners to mirror VoiceOver behavior and handle custom actions:
textdocument.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  if (e.ctrlKey && e.altKey) { // Detect VO modifier
    if (e.key === 'ArrowRight' || e.key === 'ArrowDown') {
      moveToNextSibling();
      updateAriaLive('Moved to next item');
    } else if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft' || e.key === 'ArrowUp') {
      moveToPreviousSibling();
      updateAriaLive('Moved to previous item');
    } else if (e.shiftKey && e.key === 'ArrowDown') {
      expandNode(focusedNode);
      updateAriaLive('Entered child level');
    } else if (e.shiftKey && e.key === 'ArrowUp') {
      collapseNode(focusedNode);
      updateAriaLive('Exited to parent level');
    } else if (e.key === ' ') { // Space for activation
      jumpToSection(focusedNode.dataset.turnId);
      updateAriaLive('Jumped to conversation turn: ' + focusedNode.ariaLabel);
      // Optional TTS read of GPT content
      readGptContent(focusedNode);
    }
  }
});

Cursor Prompt: "Implement ARIA tree view keyboard navigation in Chrome extension JS, with VoiceOver shortcuts for sibling (VO + arrows) and hierarchical (VO + Shift + Up/Down) movement on macOS."

Announcements and TTS Assistance

Jump Confirmation: After scrollIntoView, use aria-live to announce "ChatGPT conversation scrolled to relevant content: [headline]. Ready to read."
Auxiliary Reading: On selection, prompt TTS for GPT content:

Use SpeechSynthesis API: const utterance = new SpeechSynthesisUtterance(gptText); speechSynthesis.speak(utterance);
Start with summary: "Summary: [keypoints]. Press VO + Space for full content."
Pause/Resume: Add buttons with aria-controls linking to TTS.


Voice Mode: Toggle enables auto-announce on focus (e.g., VO reads node on arrow move).

Fullscreen Mode Accessibility

Top-Level Structure: Define summaries as highest-level headings (e.g., Headline: 项目概述) for rotor navigation (VO + U > Headings).
Hierarchical Jumps: Use  for sub-content, allowing VO + Shift + Down to enter keypoints/response.
Focus Shift: On fullscreen activation, move focus to top-level h2; jumps use focus() to target sections, triggering VO read.

Integration with Extension Architecture

Content Script: Handle DOM jumps (scrollIntoView) and extract GPT text for TTS.
Side Panel/Full-Screen: Apply ARIA to HTML; message passing for announcements (chrome.runtime.sendMessage({action: 'announce', text: 'Jumped to...'})).
Fallback: If VoiceOver not detected, default to standard keyboard.

Testing Guidelines

Tools: VoiceOver (Cmd + F5 on Mac), axe DevTools extension for ARIA checks.
Test Cases:

Navigate outline: Use VO + arrows for siblings, confirm reads "Tree item, level 1, collapsed, 项目概述".
Hierarchical: VO + Shift + Down enters children (announces "Entered level 2, 3 keypoints"), VO + Shift + Up exits.
Expand/Jump: VO + Space expands/jumps (announces "Expanded" or "Jumped to turn 3").
TTS: Select node, confirm reads keypoints/GPT summary.
Full Flow: From activation to jump and read, no interruptions.


Metrics: 0 critical WCAG violations (axe scan); VoiceOver navigation time <10s for 20 nodes.
Iteration: Record VoiceOver session, fix if announcements overlap or miss.
